      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wilbraham first drew attention in 1636 when William Pynchon bought the area from the Nipmuc, extending to the foot of the Wilbraham Mountain Range by 1674.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilbraham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Underground Railroad threaded through Wilbraham, with several Main Street houses and Wilbraham Mountain homes serving as secret stations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilbraham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wilbraham hosts New England’s oldest Methodist meeting house and the Wilbraham & Monson Academy, founded in 1804, both anchoring the town’s historic center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilbraham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rice’s Fruit Farm, opened in 1894, remains a historic New England family‑run stand offering breakfast, coffee, pies, apples and ice cream.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilbraham%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Wilbraham’s name traces back to Wilburga’s homestead, the daughter of King Penda, whose land gave the town’s original designation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
